<Layout title="Start here — Elie Schulman" description="A short reading path through language, interpretation, and what happens when people understand the same words differently.">
  <header class="page-header"><div class="page-header-inner"><p class="eyebrow">A place to begin</p><h1>One question, several ways in.</h1><p class="lede">What have we assumed we understand?</p></div></header>
  <div class="content">
    <p>You can begin with any of these. Taken together, they move from the words on a page toward the exchange between people.</p>
    <h2>1. Begin with an exchange</h2><p><a href="/writing/when-explaining-becomes-defending/">When explaining becomes defending</a> begins with the difference between being understood and being agreed with.</p>
    <h2>2. Notice who supplies the meaning</h2><p><a href="/books/vocabulary-pov/">Read the book on vocabulary and point of view</a>. Stay with a word you use easily, and consider what becomes visible when someone else uses it differently.</p>
    <h2>3. Bring another person into the question</h2><p><a href="/writing/when-nobody-fills-the-silence/">What happens when nobody fills the silence</a> asks what we can discover by hearing from the people whose experience we were already explaining.</p>
    <p>There is no need to finish a book before beginning a conversation.</p>
    <h2>Receive new writing</h2><p>For essays and reflections on language, Torah, judgment, and learning, <a href="https://emesnewsletter.substack.com/subscribe">subscribe to my newsletter</a>.</p>
  </div>
  <CohortInvitation source="start_here" />
</Layout>
